==English==
{{wikipedia|Extraversion and introversion}}
===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|extravert}} {{q|proper}}

===Etymology===
A misspelling of {{m|en|extravert}}, popularized in psychology by Phyllis Blanchard's 1918 "Psycho-Analytic Study of August Comte", equivalent to {{prefix|en|extro-|vert}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈɛkstrəvəːt/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈɛkstrəˌvərt/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-extrovert.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɜː(ɹ)t|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} {{altform|extroverted|lang=en|nodot=a}}: [[outgoing]].

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} An [[extroverted]] [[person]]: one who is [[outgoing]], [[sociable]], and [[concern]]ed with [[outer]] [[affairs]].

====Usage notes====
Technical papers in psychology overwhelmingly prefer the original and more proper {{m|en|extravert}}, although the misspelling ''extrovert'' has become more common in general use.

====Antonyms====
* {{l|en|introvert}}

